    "readout_answer": "45",
    "readout_text": "To determine the degree measure of the acute angle formed by two lines with slopes $ m_1 = 2 $ and $ m_2 = \\frac{1}{3} $, we use the standard formula for the angle $ \\theta $ between two lines:\n\n$$\n\\tan(\\theta) = \\left| \\frac{m_2 - m_1}{1 + m_1 m_2} \\right|\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Plug in the values\n\n$$\n\\tan(\\theta) = \\left| \\frac{\\frac{1}{3} - 2}{1 + 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}} \\right|\n$$\n\n$$\n= \\left| \\frac{-\\frac{5}{3}}{\\frac{5}{3}} \\right| = \\left| -1 \\right| = 1\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Compute the angle\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arctan(1)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\theta = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince $ \\tan(\\theta) = 1 $, the angle is $ 45^\\circ $, which is the acute angle between the two lines.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Confirm the result\n\nWe can also verify this by considering the angles each line makes with the x-axis:\n\n- $ \\alpha = \\arctan(2) \\approx 63.43^\\circ $\n- $ \\beta = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\approx 18.43^\\circ $\n\nThe angle between the two lines is:\n\n$$\n|\\alpha - \\beta| = 63.43^\\circ - 18.43^\\circ = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\nThis confirms that the acute angle is indeed $ 45^\\circ $.\n\n---\n\n### Conclusion\n\nThe formula and multiple approaches (including vector dot product and angle subtraction) all lead to the same conclusion. The acute angle between the two lines is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{45}\n$$\n\n\\confidence{Almost certain}",
